================================================================================
PART 4: UNDERSTANDING filter()
================================================================================

WHAT DOES filter() DO?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

filter() examines each array element and returns a new array containing only
the elements for which the callback returns:

true


Example:

const items = [

  {
    id: 1,
    name: 'Milk'
  },

  {
    id: 2,
    name: 'Bread'
  },

  {
    id: 3,
    name: 'Apples'
  }

]


const remainingItems =
  items.filter(
    item =>
      item.id !== 2
  )


Result:

[
  {
    id: 1,
    name: 'Milk'
  },

  {
    id: 3,
    name: 'Apples'
  }
]


Original:

items


remains unchanged.


================================================================================
HOW filter() PROCESSES THE ARRAY
================================================================================

Target:

id = 2


Milk:

1 !== 2

true

KEEP


Bread:

2 !== 2

false

REMOVE


Apples:

3 !== 2

true

KEEP


Result:

Milk

Apples


================================================================================
filter() DOES NOT MEAN "DELETE"
================================================================================

filter() does not directly delete an array element.

It creates:

A NEW ARRAY


containing only items satisfying the condition.


For deletion, we design the condition so the unwanted item is excluded.


MEMORY:

filter()
 ↓
KEEP WHAT MATCHES THE CONDITION


Therefore:

item.id !== id


means:

KEEP EVERYTHING EXCEPT THE ITEM
WE WANT TO REMOVE.


================================================================================
filter() VS splice()
================================================================================

+----------------------+----------------------+------------------------------+
| ASPECT               | filter()             | splice()                     |
+----------------------+----------------------+------------------------------+
| Original Modified?   | No                   | Yes                          |
| Result               | New array            | Mutates source + returns     |
|                      |                      | removed elements             |
| State Update Pattern | Natural fit          | Avoid direct state mutation  |
| Intent Here          | Keep non-targets     | Remove by array position     |
+----------------------+----------------------+------------------------------+


================================================================================
WHY NOT splice() DIRECTLY ON STATE?
================================================================================

Avoid:

groceryItems.splice(
  index,
  1
)


because this directly modifies the array stored in React state.


Instead:

const remaining =
  groceryItems.filter(
    item =>
      item.id !== id
  )


creates the next array without modifying the existing one.


================================================================================
DELETE BY ID VS DELETE BY INDEX
================================================================================

Prefer targeting:

item.id


rather than:

array index


Why?

An item can move because of:

• Filtering

• Sorting

• Adding items

• Removing items


Its identity should remain:

item.id


not:

its current visual array position.


